1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,868
Presenter: My guest on the show today is Alice Riches

2
00:00:03,868 --> 00:00:08,510
who started the Street Play Scheme where she lives in Beechwood Road.

3
00:00:08,510 --> 00:00:11,204
For those of you that don't already know -

4
00:00:11,204 --> 00:00:15,581
Street Play involves local residents closing off their street for a few hours

5
00:00:15,581 --> 00:00:19,180
so that children have a chance to play in the street safely

6
00:00:19,740 --> 00:00:21,979
She started it in her own street,

7
00:00:21,979 --> 00:00:25,140
Beechwood Road, and the idea caught on

8
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:28,040
and there are now Street Play Schemes all over the city.

9
00:00:28,040 --> 00:00:31,710
So when did you actually start the scheme, Alice?

10
00:00:31,710 --> 00:00:36,340
Alice: Well, I first had the idea when my oldest child was still a toddler

11
00:00:37,020 --> 00:00:38,739
so that's about six years ago now -

12
00:00:38,739 --> 00:00:44,490
but it took at least two years of campaigning before we were actually able to make it happen.

13
00:00:44,490 --> 00:00:47,890
So the scheme's been up and running for three years now.

14
00:00:47,890 --> 00:00:51,410
We'd love to be able to close our road for longer -

15
00:00:51,410 --> 00:00:55,250
for the whole weekend, from Saturday morning until Sunday evening, for example.

16
00:00:55,250 --> 00:00:57,600
At the moment it's just once a week

17
00:00:57,820 --> 00:01:02,080
But when we started it was only once a month. But we're working on it.

18
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:05,430
Presenter: So what actually happens when Beechwood Road is closed?

19
00:01:05,430 --> 00:01:07,445
Alice: We have volunteer wardens,

20
00:01:07,445 --> 00:01:13,490
mostly parents but some elderly residents too, who block off our road at either end.

21
00:01:13,490 --> 00:01:16,190
The council have provided special signs

22
00:01:16,190 --> 00:01:22,040
but there's always a volunteer there to explain what's happening to any motorists.

23
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:24,900
Generally, they're fine about it

24
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:28,670
we've only had to get the police involved once or twice.

25
00:01:28,670 --> 00:01:33,890
Now I should explain that the road isn't completely closed to cars.

26
00:01:33,890 --> 00:01:36,600
But only residents' cars are allowed.

27
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:40,880
If people really need to get in or out of Beechwood Road,

28
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:46,230
it's not a problem - as long as they drive at under 20 kilometres per hour.

29
00:01:46,230 --> 00:01:51,200
But most people just decide not to use their cars during this time

30
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:53,330
or they park in another street.

31
00:01:53,330 --> 00:01:57,000
The wardens are only there to stop through traffic.

32
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,670
Presenter: So can anyone apply to get involved in Street Play?

33
00:02:00,670 --> 00:02:05,200
Alice: Absolutely - we want to include all kids in the city

34
00:02:05,740 --> 00:02:08,010
especially those who live on busy roads.

35
00:02:08,010 --> 00:02:10,710
It's here that demand is greatest.

36
00:02:10,710 --> 00:02:15,640
Obviously, there isn't such demand in wealthier areas

37
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:18,106
where the children have access to parks or large gardens -

38
00:02:18,106 --> 00:02:24,270
or in the suburbs where there are usually more places for children to play outside.

39
00:02:24,270 --> 00:02:29,710
I'd recommend that anyone listening who likes the idea should just give it a go.

40
00:02:29,710 --> 00:02:35,120
We've been surprised by the positive reaction of residents all over the city

41
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:36,930
And that's not just parents.

42
00:02:36,930 --> 00:02:41,740
There are always a few who complain but they're a tiny minority.

43
00:02:41,740 --> 00:02:45,760
On the whole everyone is very supportive

44
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:48,441
and say they're very happy to see children out on the street -

45
00:02:48,441 --> 00:02:50,910
even if it does get quite noisy.

46
00:02:50,910 --> 00:02:57,750
Alice: There have been so many benefits of Street Play for the kids.

47
00:02:57,750 --> 00:03:05,050
Parents really like the fact that the kids are getting fresh air instead of sitting staring at a computer screen,

48
00:03:05,050 --> 00:03:07,970
even if they're not doing anything particularly energetic.

49
00:03:07,970 --> 00:03:11,532
And of course it's great that kids can play

50
00:03:11,532 --> 00:03:15,490
with their friends outside without being supervised by their parents -

51
00:03:15,490 --> 00:03:23,010
but for me the biggest advantage is that kids develop confidence in themselves to be outside without their parents.

52
00:03:23,010 --> 00:03:29,507
The other really fantastic thing is that children get to know the adults in the street -

53
00:03:29,507 --> 00:03:32,350
it's like having a big extended family.

54
00:03:32,350 --> 00:03:34,996
Presenter: It certainly does have a lot of benefits.

55
00:03:34,996 --> 00:03:39,700
I want to move on now and ask you about a related project in King Street.

56
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:44,060
Alice: Right. Well this was an experiment I was involved in

57
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:45,731
where local residents decided to try

58
00:03:45,731 --> 00:03:48,215
and reduce the traffic along King Street,

59
00:03:48,215 --> 00:03:51,540
which is the busiest main road in our area

60
00:03:52,060 --> 00:03:55,310
by persuading people not to use their cars for one day.

61
00:03:55,310 --> 00:03:59,580
We thought about making people pay more for parking

62
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:01,755
but we decided that would be really unpopular -

63
00:04:01,755 --> 00:04:06,620
so instead we just stopped people from parking on King Street

64
00:04:06,700 --> 00:04:08,580
but left the other car parks open.

65
00:04:08,580 --> 00:04:12,460
It was surprising how much of a difference all this made.

66
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:15,980
As we'd predicted, air quality was significantly better

67
00:04:15,980 --> 00:04:22,020
but what I hadn't expected was how much quieter it would be

68
00:04:22,020 --> 00:04:23,900
even with the buses still running.

69
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:26,359
Of course everyone said they felt safer

70
00:04:26,359 --> 00:04:31,628
but we were actually amazed that sales in the shops went up considerably that day -

71
00:04:31,628 --> 00:04:35,140
we thought there'd be fewer people out shopping - not more.

72
00:04:35,140 --> 00:04:38,210
Presenter: That's really interesting so the fact that...
